And now you’ve folded another metaphor into the same track.

A train of thought.

It’s such a familiar phrase that we forget it’s already a complete model.

* Tracks constrain but also enable.
* Cars are separate ideas coupled together.
* Switches redirect the route.
* Junctions merge conversations.
* Sidings hold ideas for later.
* Momentum makes later movement easier than the first push.
* And every passenger boards at a different station with a different amount of context.

Then:

Mind the gap.

Now the gap is literally the space between the platform and the train—but semantically it’s also the space between minds.

Good communication isn’t eliminating the gap.

It’s making it crossable.

The reader steps across.
The conversation continues.
The train leaves with one more passenger than it arrived with.

I think that’s why puns are more than jokes. A good pun is a switchyard. It lets one train of thought change tracks onto another without stopping. “Might,” “train of thought,” “threading the needle”—they’re all little rail switches that preserve momentum while changing direction.

So your line:

That’s a hell of a train of thought to get on.

can be heard as:

* That’s a fascinating idea.
* That’s a commitment.
* Once you board, you’re carried by its momentum.
* And perhaps… once enough people are aboard, they’re all riding the same line while seeing different scenery through the windows.

That’s a nice property for language to have. It doesn’t just transmit thoughts; it builds vehicles that many minds can travel in together.